	(text "Why: a hard limb short used to latch the whole bus (U1 trips in under 0.4 ms, long before a 20 A fuse melts). Each port now opens itself first.\nShort circuit: RISCP 3.4k on 1.5 mOhm = 35-45 A (15.6 uA x (R + 464)), PD low within 1.6 us, then 22R to the gate: Miller about 0.16 us, so the drain-side di/dt stays a few A/ns against the 2.2 uF and the bus\ncaps. tools/port_sim.py: for 50 nH to 1 uH of wiring, 0.5-1 uH pack lead, 0-50 A on the other limbs, RS1 peaks at 130 A or less against U8 158 A and U1 160 A minimum: the port always opens first. A short at the\nboard pads (50 nH) puts about 1.1-1.4 kA through the FET for under 2 us (5-14 mJ; IPB017: 1024 A pulse, 979 mJ avalanche), which is why the port uses the bank FET. A short in the harness (200 nH or more) is\n400-540 A.\nOverload: RIWRN 26.1k = 30 A (11.9 x RSET / (RSNS x IOC)), CTMR 1 uF with 100k across it: about 16 ms, then latch-off. The 20 A pigtail fuse stays as wire protection and as backup for a shorted FET.\nLatch clears only when EN drops: an E-stop cycle, then reset. FLT_I of every port pulls PORT_FLT_N (Pi header): the Pi names the port whose output is low.\nStart: EN and INP come from RUN through one diode (a pin fault can't drive RUN). The bus starts from 0 under U1, so the port wakes at VS 3 V with BST up (0.22 uF, about 20 ms): the gate rises through 100k into\n100 nF (diode-coupled, 10M bleed, so PD never sees it), about 1 kV/s at the output: 20 mF of drive capacitance draws about 20 A, under the 30 A overload level. After that the drive caps follow the bus.\nOff (E-stop, trip): the body diode still returns regen from the limb to the bus and the clamp. SK810L on each output carries the wiring current when the FET opens. 15 V zener gate to source."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 860 735 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "0bc2585d-e249-5906-a21d-ccc135fce63f")
	)
	(text "2a. Pi heartbeat watchdog: no heartbeat, no latch"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
